03:08:55.199 --> 03:08:58.739
So side-effecting expressions in C.

03:08:58.739 --> 03:09:02.639
I have int x equals one and then I try to print.

03:09:02.639 --> 03:09:06.719
After this plus plus business here, that's a pre-increment.

03:09:06.719 --> 03:09:10.180
So it's going to increment x first, and then print two.

03:09:10.180 --> 03:09:12.600
The value of x is now two, and that's a side effect.

03:09:12.600 --> 03:09:15.440
You change something in memory.

03:09:15.440 --> 03:09:21.180
Here if I have int x equals one, if I do that, and I try to print it,

03:09:21.180 --> 03:09:25.180
it will print one and post increment.

03:09:25.180 --> 03:09:30.500
But once this statement finishes, there is a side effect of x getting

03:09:30.500 --> 03:09:33.899
incremented to two.

03:09:33.899 --> 03:09:38.819
Next one, so x equals 1 plus y equals 5.

03:09:38.819 --> 03:09:41.739
This is going to assign 5 to y.

03:09:41.739 --> 03:09:46.520
And so then the whole value of, so in c, when you do this assignment,

03:09:46.520 --> 03:09:50.860
the value of that expression is going to be 5.

03:09:50.860 --> 03:09:53.620
And then we're going to say 1 plus 5, so x will get 6.

03:09:55.180 --> 03:09:58.079
And the value of that whole expression is 6.

03:09:58.500 --> 03:10:01.739
And even though we don't save it anywhere, it does evaluate to 6.

03:10:04.680 --> 03:10:07.059
So this one here is a problem.

03:10:07.399 --> 03:10:12.380
So if x equals 1, and then I have this business in here, so what am I doing?

03:10:12.520 --> 03:10:19.579
I'm going to say x equals x plus 1, and then add x, and then print it.

03:10:20.159 --> 03:10:21.559
There's no sequence point.

03:10:21.659 --> 03:10:22.659
This is undefined.

03:10:23.120 --> 03:10:24.280
So what's a sequence point?

03:10:24.280 --> 03:10:31.940
Well, a sequence point is a point in the execution of a C program at which all previous side effects are guaranteed to be complete.

03:10:32.420 --> 03:10:34.079
So in this case, we don't know.

03:10:34.840 --> 03:10:37.500
The language doesn't define what the order is.

03:10:38.300 --> 03:10:40.020
And so we don't know what happens.

03:10:42.120 --> 03:10:46.079
And so in the C language, it might compile.

03:10:46.420 --> 03:10:48.560
It might complain and not compile.

03:10:49.420 --> 03:10:50.239
It depends on the compiler.

03:10:50.239 --> 03:10:57.020
So side effects are very common in object-oriented programming.

03:10:57.020 --> 03:11:03.059
Here, I have some, I think it's C++ or Java code,

03:11:03.059 --> 03:11:06.360
it's probably Java code, it's probably Java code.

03:11:06.360 --> 03:11:08.600
So here, I'm updating x, right?

03:11:08.600 --> 03:11:13.440
I have this private in x, changing x to have x minus c.

03:11:13.440 --> 03:11:15.840
If I do that, I had a side effect,

03:11:15.840 --> 03:11:18.559
it changed something in memory.

03:11:18.559 --> 03:11:19.779
Same with g with y.

03:11:23.180 --> 03:11:26.939
So the results are often going to depend on the object state and

03:11:26.939 --> 03:11:29.039
potentially on the entire execution history.

03:11:29.039 --> 03:11:30.859
Cuz you don't know what it was called before, right?

03:11:30.859 --> 03:11:31.760
.

03:11:31.760 --> 03:11:37.779
So in c, if we wanna sequence expressions,

03:11:37.779 --> 03:11:40.459
we can use this comma operator.

03:11:40.459 --> 03:11:45.379
So if I have a statement sequence in c, this is what it looks like.

03:11:45.379 --> 03:11:48.359
This is a statement in x equals 5.

03:11:48.359 --> 03:11:49.459
x times equal 2.

03:11:49.459 --> 03:11:50.959
Notice the semicolon, right?

03:11:50.959 --> 03:11:54.579
So we have statement, statement, and then a printf expression

03:11:54.579 --> 03:11:58.039
within a statement.

03:11:58.039 --> 03:12:03.139
We can do an expression sequence this way with these commas.

03:12:03.139 --> 03:12:04.459
Sorry, these commas here.

03:12:04.459 --> 03:12:09.039
So here I have x times equal 2.

03:12:09.039 --> 03:12:12.319
Now, that comma means that in the language,

03:12:12.319 --> 03:12:19.940
this will happen before this gets passed and spliced in here at the printf.

03:12:19.940 --> 03:12:26.260
This comma means that this defines the fact that this had to have,

03:12:26.260 --> 03:12:31.840
the side effect has to have, executed and completed before this is accessed and used.

03:12:36.299 --> 03:12:37.360
So here's our summary.

03:12:39.239 --> 03:12:40.920
Statements are gonna change memory.

03:12:42.319 --> 03:12:44.539
They are executed in sequence.

03:12:46.139 --> 03:12:50.719
Expressions can be pure, and they can also side effect.

03:12:53.879 --> 03:12:58.659
You can sequence by an operator in the expression language.

03:12:59.340 --> 03:13:04.680
So in C, we have expression one comma expression two comma up to some other.

03:13:04.680 --> 03:13:12.460
And we can do a conditional by operator in an expression language like this.

03:13:12.579 --> 03:13:16.180
So you can see we have some expression here, question mark.

03:13:16.639 --> 03:13:20.680
